All the lines after the $ should be written by the user in the command line.

* Edit the input file, SrTiO3.fdf, and study the different variables.

* Run the code for a set of different lattice constant,
embracing the minimum of the energy.
Save the output of each run in a different file.
That is, for each lattice constant run a command like this:

$ siesta < SrTiO3.fdf > SrTiO3.3.70.out &
tail -f SrTiO3.3.70.out

In this example, you can try with
3.70 Ang
3.75 Ang
3.80 Ang
3.85 Ang
3.90 Ang
3.95 Ang
4.00 Ang
4.05 Ang

* Save in a file the data needed to plot a figure energy 
versus lattice constant.
In order to get this, type:

$ grep "Total =" SrTiO3.*.out > SrTiO3.evslc.dat

* Edit the file SrTiO3.evslc.dat with your favorite text editor and
keep only the information of the lattice constant (in Ang) and 
energy (in eV). 
Introduce in the first line of this file the type of Bravais lattice
of the system under study (sc, bcc, fcc, or diamond).
For the present example, Si, it would be diamond
It should look like something like:

cubic
3.70   -3738.617262      
3.75   -3739.029827      
3.80   -3739.282341      
3.85   -3739.395325      
3.90   -3739.396974      
3.95   -3739.295355      
4.00   -3739.088046      
4.05   -3738.806515      

* Run the script to fit the energy versus volume data to the
Murnaghan equation of state.

If you are running SIESTA MOBILE on the virtual machine,
type

$ workon siesta_school

to upload the environment of python will all the modules installed.

Then,

$ python fit_results.py SrTiO3.evslc.dat

The result is a plot with the calculated points,
the fitting curve and the values of the volume at the minimum (in Angstrom^3),
the bulk modulus (in eV/Ang^3; Remember that 1 eV/Ang^3 = 160.2176487 GPa), 
the energy at the minimum (in eV),
and the pressure derivative of the bulk modulus at the equilibrium volume. 

A figure can be automatically generated writting the name of the destination
file as a final argument in the in-line command. Several formats are
allowed (pdf, gif, jpeg,...)

$ python fit_results.py SrTiO3.evslc.dat SrTiO3.evsvol.pdf

NOTE: To run the script in python, you need to install
the following modules in python:

Numpy 
Scipy
Matplotlib

All these packages are automatically included in the anaconda distribution,
that is freely available and easy to install from
https://store.continuum.io/cshop/anaconda/


